              </span>
              <h3>The situation</h3>
            </div>
            <p>A young couple putting together a used £18,500 hatchback on PCP. They'd test driven on Wednesday, finance went out Thursday, deposit paid Friday. Handover booked for Saturday at 11. The team thought everything was in order.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-card cc-alert">
            <div class="cc-head">
              <span class="cc-icon">
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"/><line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/></svg>
              </span>
              <h3>What Drew Dealership Admin caught</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The proof of address on file was 5 months old — finance house wouldn't accept anything over 3. The part-ex V5C had only the husband's name; the new finance was in joint names. The MOT on the unit being sold expired in 4 weeks. It then drafted the customer chase and the workshop note.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-card cc-success">
            <div class="cc-head">
              <span class="cc-icon">
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
              </span>
              <h3>The outcome</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The sales exec sent the customer the address and V5C requests on Thursday evening. Both came back the next morning. Workshop fitted a fresh MOT Friday afternoon. Finance approved Saturday at 9. Handover ran to time at 11. No "we'll have to delay this 30 minutes" awkwardness — and a couple who told their friends.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
